shall we?

40
00:04:09,040 --> 00:04:24,480
Over these next three podcasts, we're going to be talking about the three most challenging

41
00:04:24,480 --> 00:04:29,360
things that happen in scouting so far as parents are concerned, And this week I want to begin

42
00:04:29,360 --> 00:04:34,960
by talking to parents about whether or not they should compel a reluctant child to keep

43
00:04:34,960 --> 00:04:40,239
going on in scouting, because this is a question that I get a lot, and there's a number of circumstances

44
00:04:40,239 --> 00:04:45,599
that conspire that make this a kind of a really difficult issue for a lot of parents. Let's

45
00:04:45,599 --> 00:04:50,479
set the stage for a moment here. First of all, the age of the children that I'm thinking about

46
00:04:50,479 --> 00:04:55,280
as we talk about this are children between the ages of, let's say, 11 and 15, so the

47
00:04:55,280 --> 00:05:00,640
adolescent years, which can be particularly tumultuous. It's a great time of life in a lot

48
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:04,960
of ways, and it's a really hard time of life in a lot of other ways, especially if you're

49
00:05:04,960 --> 00:05:10,400
the parent of an adolescent child, Even if you've already been through this with an older child.

50
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:16,880
every child is individual enough that these years are going to be at least slightly different-

51
00:05:16,880 --> 00:05:21,919
and many times dramatically different- between children even in the same family. If you

52
00:05:21,919 --> 00:05:26,240
are going through this for the first time as a parent, it can be really, really confusing.

53
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:31,680
So the advice I'm going to offer you is based on more than 30 years of volunteering with children

54
00:05:31,680 --> 00:05:38,480
this age and being able to observe children go through their adolescence hundreds of times

55
00:05:38,480 --> 00:05:44,240
and see them on the other side, which is really hard to picture. sometimes, when you're having

56
00:05:44,240 --> 00:05:48,400
difficulty with your own child, It's kind of hard to picture that you're going to get

57
00:05:48,400 --> 00:05:55,040
through all of this- because 99.99% of us do- and you're going to be on the other side

58
00:05:55,040 --> 00:06:00,800
watching hundreds of parents and children go through this. I hope that you'll value some of

59
00:06:00,800 --> 00:06:05,680
what I have to say. So, before we even talk about Scouts, let's just get an idea of what children

60
00:06:05,680 --> 00:06:11,120
need at this age, and this shouldn't be news to anybody. They need some very basic, simple things.

61
00:06:11,120 --> 00:06:16,080
They need consistency and security, They need some discipline and expectations

62
00:06:16,080 --> 00:06:20,560
and they need a lot of affirmation and confidence. When you're an adolescent,

63
00:06:20,560 --> 00:06:26,960
your world is shifting on you. Things are changing, You are changing, Your mind is changing,

64
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:32,720
Everything around and the world- and it looks like the world around you- is changing at the same time.

65
00:06:32,720 --> 00:06:39,280
You're very sensitive to things like peer pressure. You're very sensitive to negative experiences.

66
00:06:39,280 --> 00:06:45,760
You're very sensitive to positive experiences and you have very little life experience,

67
00:06:45,760 --> 00:06:51,680
So your decision-making skills are not going to be all that sharp and your ability to look into

68
00:06:51,680 --> 00:06:59,360
your own future and to make decisions based in your best interest is not going to be a very strong

69
00:06:59,360 --> 00:07:04,400
set of skills when you're an adolescent. Thankfully, you have parents and you have

70
00:07:04,400 --> 00:07:09,280
caring adults around you who are going to help advise you through these two

71
00:07:09,280 --> 00:07:15,440
multiple years, and hopefully you will listen to them and things will go well. While all of that

72
00:07:15,440 --> 00:07:20,880
is true, it is also true that when you're an adolescent, you have to start to learn to make

73
00:07:20,880 --> 00:07:26,320
your own decisions and make your own way. I mean, do you remember how confusing and unsettling

74
00:07:26,320 --> 00:07:32,400
that time of life could be? So, basically, you have to take a deep breath, step back a moment

75
00:07:32,400 --> 00:07:36,640
and observe what's going on, and that'll help you find your feet as a parent.

76
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:41,760
So you are the parent of a child who has been a scout for a while- It could be a number of months

77
00:07:41,760 --> 00:07:45,919
or a number of years- and they come to you and they say: I don't want to do scouts anymore.

78
00:07:45,919 --> 00:07:54,560
So my first little bit of advice is: do not overreact. okay And best of luck. I understand

79
00:07:54,560 --> 00:07:59,680
I can be a very reactive person myself, but don't overreact. Just listen And then triage

80
00:07:59,680 --> 00:08:05,520
one or two quick things. Has your scout had a bad experience of some kind and does it raise

81
00:08:05,520 --> 00:08:10,799
to the level of seriousness where it needs the attention of the adults who are working with

82
00:08:10,799 --> 00:08:15,840
them and scouts? Have they been bullied? Had another negative experience that needs to be

83
00:08:15,840 --> 00:08:22,159
corrected? So triage that first, Just check on that and then listen very carefully to their

84
00:08:22,159 --> 00:08:28,159
reasoning. They may not have the best understanding of exactly why they're feeling the way that

85
00:08:28,159 --> 00:08:34,079
they're feeling. At this point I would not issue a verdict. I would not make a decision

86
00:08:34,080 --> 00:08:39,920
other than to say to the child: okay, well, I've heard what you had to say and we're going to either

87
00:08:39,920 --> 00:08:46,560
fix the situation- and I'm glad that you told me- or let's see how you feel about this again

88
00:08:46,560 --> 00:08:52,240
next week And this might go away on its own and it might come back up. But we've given

89
00:08:52,240 --> 00:08:58,320
ourselves and our child some latitude to get away from the heat of the moment and begin to

90
00:08:58,320 --> 00:09:03,280
examine this decision with a level head. So what if they come back to you again and they say:

91
00:09:03,280 --> 00:09:07,839
you know, I really don't want to be in scouts anymore. Thought about it. We talked the last time.

92
00:09:07,839 --> 00:09:13,520
Still the same reasons, Not very happy doing it. Well, it's not unreasonable that a child in their

93
00:09:13,520 --> 00:09:18,160
adolescence might be ready to move on to something else. Maybe they have another interest or something

94
00:09:18,160 --> 00:09:22,800
that has taken the place of scouts and they want to devote a lot of time to it and pursue it.

95
00:09:22,800 --> 00:09:26,720
That's very valid. You know, is it something they took up just a little while ago or is it

96
00:09:26,720 --> 00:09:32,400
something that's been growing? And so at that point, faced with that decision and helping to

97
00:09:32,400 --> 00:09:38,480
advise a child about that, I would say, okay, well, you don't have to make the jump right now.

98
00:09:38,480 --> 00:09:44,240
Let's see how this plays out for a given period of time- And you know, maybe that's three weeks

99
00:09:44,240 --> 00:09:50,240
or three months- and see if this new interest has some staying power And if it does

100
00:09:50,240 --> 00:09:55,439
no harm, no foul, Let's move on to it and let's invest in it and pay attention to it.

101
00:09:55,439 --> 00:10:00,000
As a scouter, I don't have a problem with that. And if you're a parent and you're in the

102
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:03,680
throes of this kind of situation with your scout, come and talk to us. Talk to the

103
00:10:03,680 --> 00:10:07,920
scouters who are working with your child. Let them know what's going on. They may have a

104
00:10:07,920 --> 00:10:12,640
perspective on it that you might find helpful. Now, if your child persists in the idea

105
00:10:12,640 --> 00:10:16,320
I really don't want to do scouts anymore, They don't have another interest that they want

106
00:10:16,320 --> 00:10:21,120
to pursue. And I want to reiterate again: if it's a sudden change, if it's like a night

107
00:10:21,120 --> 00:10:24,720
and day change, if it's like somebody threw a switch, it's more than likely something

108
00:10:24,720 --> 00:10:28,320
negative has happened and you really need to follow up on that. But if they cannot

109
00:10:28,320 --> 00:10:32,480
articulate what is making them feel this way, why they want to discontinue being

110
00:10:32,480 --> 00:10:37,440
involved with scouts, my advice to you as a parent is just to keep on going and to

111
00:10:37,440 --> 00:10:43,520
keep them involved, and even to require them to be involved, Because, as we said a

112
00:10:43,520 --> 00:10:48,880
moment ago, they need consistency and security in what they're doing. They need

113
00:10:48,880 --> 00:10:53,360
affirmation and confidence and they need discipline and expectations. They don't

114
00:10:53,360 --> 00:10:59,520
particularly like any of those things, But it's your job as a parent to be consistent

115
00:10:59,520 --> 00:11:04,800
and just to stick with it. The adults working with your child and scouts know

116
00:11:04,800 --> 00:11:08,640
that this is going on because they're going to observe things that you won't.

117
00:11:08,640 --> 00:11:15,520
So let me go back 10 or 15 years and think about our own boy being in scouts

118
00:11:15,520 --> 00:11:19,600
and his reluctance at times to keep going, to keep moving on.

119
00:11:19,600 --> 00:11:24,240
You know, some of his relationships with his friends shifted a little bit.

120
00:11:24,240 --> 00:11:28,960
Some of them grew stronger, some of them got a little weaker. His love for camping

121
00:11:28,960 --> 00:11:32,640
kind of came and went, depending on who was going on the camping trip,

122
00:11:32,640 --> 00:11:39,040
But his mother's response remained very consistent: This is something that you do

123
00:11:39,040 --> 00:11:43,200
and you're going to continue doing it. Now, none of his objections were

124
00:11:43,200 --> 00:11:46,880
things that raised any serious concerns on our part.

125
00:11:46,880 --> 00:11:50,800
They were very serious to him and we understood that.

126
00:11:50,800 --> 00:11:54,560
But in the grand scheme of things we also understood

127
00:11:54,560 --> 00:11:58,080
he needed consistency, discipline and expectations

128
00:11:58,080 --> 00:12:02,000
from us, because the rest of his world was kind of shifting around.

129
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:05,200
Now I've advised a lot of other parents the same way.

130
00:12:05,200 --> 00:12:08,800
They'll come to me and they'll say I don't know whether or not they're

131
00:12:08,800 --> 00:12:12,960
going to stick with this and I say: well, you know, it kind of comes down to

132
00:12:12,960 --> 00:12:15,920
you making a decision based on what you observe

133
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:19,599
and what you know about your child. Is their reasoning behind this just largely

134
00:12:19,599 --> 00:12:23,599
emotional? Will they follow their reasoning consistently

135
00:12:23,599 --> 00:12:26,959
over a period of time? and, like I said you know, three weeks, three months, six

136
00:12:26,959 --> 00:12:29,839
months, depending on who it is and what the situation is?

137
00:12:29,839 --> 00:12:34,160
If you take that approach, you give everybody a little time to kind of cool

138
00:12:34,160 --> 00:12:37,760
off and look at it with a level head. So I began talking about this

139
00:12:37,760 --> 00:12:43,280
by asking the question: should you compel your child to continue on in

140
00:12:43,280 --> 00:12:48,480
scouts, even when they say they don't want to? and my answer is yes, but with

141
00:12:48,480 --> 00:12:51,520
reservations, and I think I hopefully have explained them to you.

142
00:12:51,520 --> 00:12:55,600
So let me go back over this ground one more time and come up with

143
00:12:55,600 --> 00:12:59,839
probably a little bit of an oversimplified answer to a very complex question.

144
00:12:59,839 --> 00:13:03,760
First of all, understand where you are in your child's development.

145
00:13:03,760 --> 00:13:07,680
If you're in the adolescent years, things are inherently unstable and

146
00:13:07,680 --> 00:13:10,400
that's fine, That's healthy, It's not a bad thing.

147
00:13:10,480 --> 00:13:15,040
It's a little tiring sometimes for a parent, but that's not a bad thing.

148
00:13:15,040 --> 00:13:18,400
So what is going to help them see their way through

149
00:13:18,400 --> 00:13:22,000
adolescence while having some stability, feeling safe,

150
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:26,800
having some consistency, some discipline and expectations,

151
00:13:26,800 --> 00:13:29,920
affirmation and a lot of confidence is going to help them too,

152
00:13:29,920 --> 00:13:34,640
And there's a lot of all of those things available to you as a parent and

153
00:13:34,640 --> 00:13:38,319
to your child when they are involved in scouts.

154
00:13:38,320 --> 00:13:42,400
So they come to you, They say: I'm really not interested in being a scout anymore.

155
00:13:42,400 --> 00:13:45,760
You listen carefully. You don't make a judgment right there.

156
00:13:45,760 --> 00:13:50,880
You listen carefully. You suggest: okay, I'm hearing what you're saying.

157
00:13:50,880 --> 00:13:54,000
I would like to see if this stands the test of time.

158
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:58,640
Let's talk about this again in two or three weeks and see how you feel. then

159
00:13:58,640 --> 00:14:02,080
Talk to the adults who are working with your child in scouts.

160
00:14:02,080 --> 00:14:05,760
Talk to them about any possible problems your scout might be encountering

161
00:14:05,760 --> 00:14:09,840
and remember, as always, if this is like a really sudden change,

162
00:14:09,840 --> 00:14:14,000
I would be concerned that there's been some kind of a negative experience-

163
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:17,439
not to say that sudden changes always indicate that there's a negative

164
00:14:17,439 --> 00:14:20,720
experience. but it would be responsible on your part as

165
00:14:20,720 --> 00:14:24,240
a parent to just to ask enough questions of the right people to make

166
00:14:24,240 --> 00:14:27,920
sure that that is not the case. And so, once we've done that

167
00:14:27,920 --> 00:14:31,439
and once we've talked with our child about it, if they

168
00:14:31,440 --> 00:14:37,280
have consistently expressed a desire to not do scouts anymore

169
00:14:37,280 --> 00:14:41,920
and it stayed with them and they have another interest or alternative plan

170
00:14:41,920 --> 00:14:45,760
for what they're going to do rather than scouts, at least as enriching

171
00:14:45,760 --> 00:14:49,680
as their experience in scouts. like I said, no harm, no foul there.

172
00:14:49,680 --> 00:14:53,920
The other thing is if this decision not to be in scouts anymore

173
00:14:53,920 --> 00:14:58,880
seems to be either at the top or the trough of the emotional roller coaster

174
00:14:58,880 --> 00:15:02,560
that is adolescence. I usually advise parents if they ask

175
00:15:02,560 --> 00:15:07,600
to keep their children involved in scouts, and even require them to do so,

176
00:15:07,600 --> 00:15:11,600
because I've watched it any number of times and I've watched children go through

177
00:15:11,600 --> 00:15:15,280
this and see them on the other side. In my experience,

178
00:15:15,280 --> 00:15:20,320
the ones that stay in scouts appreciate their parents consistency

179
00:15:20,320 --> 00:15:23,280
later in life. That's a few thoughts about

180
00:15:23,280 --> 00:15:27,280
what can be a very complex subject, and I'm always available to talk to you

181
00:15:27,280 --> 00:15:30,240
if you're a parent or a scouter, or indeed a scout,

182
00:15:30,240 --> 00:15:34,319
and you're in the middle of a dilemma like this, and it's easy to get in touch

